FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/repl/mod.rs:1009 — format-on-enter substitutes the *reformatted* text for `code` before `execute_code_from` runs, and that same text is what every span is computed from: `render_error(&self.style, code, file, &e)` (src/repl/mod.rs:1224), `record_definitions(file, line_offset, code)` (src/repl/mod.rs:1215) and `run_program(&program, code)` → `arm_debugger(source)` (src/repl/mod.rs:1347). Verified on this tree: `printf 'if 1 is 1 then say undefined_name end\n' | ./target/debug/rb` prints
```
Error: AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'undefined_name'
  --> <session>:2:9
2 |     say undefined_name
  |         ^
```
   for a session in which that is the **only** line typed. `<session>:2` is the history line of the *next* line the user types (offset is `history.len()-1`, src/repl/mod.rs:1183) and `2 |     say undefined_name` is a line of the formatter's output that exists nowhere in the session. Same for `.which`: `if 1 is 1 then set q to 7 end` then `:which q` prints `bound at  <session>:2` while `:history` shows the `set q` on line 1 and the `:which q` command on line 2. Before this diff the same inputs reported the typed line, because the typed text was what reached these three call sites — this is an error-message regression introduced here, and neither REPORT.md nor FINDINGS.md mentions diagnostics, `.break`, or stepping at all (F9 covers only the `.which` half of the same root cause). Fix: keep the typed text for anything that carries a span — pass the original `code` to `render_error`/`record_definitions`/`set_source` and map formatted-line→typed-line, or apply format-on-enter only to what is written into the transcript and run what was typed; add a test asserting a one-line typed block's diagnostic still names line 1.
2. [MAJOR] phases/phase-060/REPORT.md:141 — "## Invariants touched … **None.**" is false, and contradicted by the report's own bullet eleven lines later (`phases/phase-060/REPORT.md:176`, "`.which` reports a definition's line in the transcript, which after format-on-enter may not be the line in the history") and by the invariant the code states as its contract at src/repl/mod.rs:1190 ("`:history` is what the user is looking up when they ask where something came from, so a span that did not match it would be a span they could not check"). A phase that breaks a documented invariant and files it under "Known gaps / follow-ups" while asserting "None" above is a report that cannot be trusted by the next reader. Fix: state the invariant, or fix finding 1 and delete the F9 bullet and the "None".
3. [MAJOR] src/repl/mod.rs:1629 — `last_file` is updated on the success path (src/repl/mod.rs:1640) but not on the `already formatted` early return, so it means neither "the last file loaded" (its doc, src/repl/mod.rs:521) nor "the last file named". Verified:
```
$ printf ':format A.rb\n:format B.rb\n:reload\n:quit\n' | ./target/debug/rb
R> Formatted 'A.rb'.
R> 'B.rb' is already formatted.
R> Loaded 13 bytes from 'A.rb'.        <- the file just named was B.rb
```
   and, in the other direction, `:format B.rb` followed by `:reload` in an empty session prints `:reload: this session has loaded no file` (src/repl/mod.rs:1569) one line after the user named `B.rb`. The field's two write sites disagree about what "last file" means, and no test covers `.format` followed by `.reload`. Fix: set `self.last_file` in `path_or_last_file`'s caller before the `needs_reformat` early return (or drop the mutation at1640 entirely), and pin the `.format` → `.reload` sequence with a test.
4. [MAJOR] src/repl/mod.rs:1545 — the new `.reload` inherits `load_file`'s message, which names no path: `Could not load file: No such file or directory (os error 2)` and, for a directory, `Could not load file: Is a directory (os error 21)`. That is exactly what the phase's own rationale for its sibling command forbids ("every failure names the path it happened at, because 'Is a directory' alone does not say which of the paths on the line was at fault", src/repl/mod.rs:1603). The failure branch of the new command has no test, and phases/phase-060/REPORT.md:159 marks the `out_of_bounds` row covered on the strength of the `.format` test alone. Fix: include the path in `reload_file`'s message (or pass it into `load_file`), and add `.reload <missing>` and `.reload <directory>` tests.
5. [MINOR] tests/repl_test.rs:1800 — `edge_format_to_a_path_that_cannot_be_written_names_the_path_and_the_session_survives` does not test writing. A directory fails at `read_to_string` (src/repl/mod.rs:1615), which prints `Could not read '<path>' to format it: Is a directory (os error 21)`; the `std::fs::write` error arm at src/repl/mod.rs:1636 is therefore never executed by any test, and phases/phase-060/REPORT.md:160's claim that this covers "a path that names a directory is not a file" describes the read path it actually reaches. Fix: add a read-only file (`chmod 0444`) case for the write arm, or rename the test to say it covers the read.
6. [MINOR] src/repl/mod.rs:1059 — `same_tokens`/`format_on_enter` have no direct test; the only coverage of the guard is `tests/repl_test.rs:1883` (rewrite path) plus, indirectly, `edge_which_names_every_name_one_import_binds`. Nothing pins the *reject* path, so an inversion of the guard — return `false` always, or drop `TokenKind::Newline` handling incorrectly — leaves the suite green with format-on-enter silently a no-op and the phase's headline feature dead. Fix: three unit tests on `Repl::format_on_enter`: an `import … as x` program returned byte-identical, a program that cannot lex returned unchanged, and `if x is 3 then say "a" end` returned unfolded.
7. [MINOR] phases/phase-060/REPORT.md:22 — the report's motivating reproduction is a program that does not run: `x   =   5` / `say x` is `AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'x'` under `rb run` both before and after formatting (verified: `rb run` on `x = 5\nsay x` and on `x is 5\nsay x` both exit 1 with the same error). The snippet therefore shows the formatter normalising layout and nothing else, and the "finding, reproduced first" section demonstrates no defect at all. Fix: use a program that runs (`if 1 is greater than 0 then … end`) as the report's own test at tests/repl_test.rs:1740 does.

